WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.960
With half-finished visual effects&nbsp;
and a stunted production schedule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:09.298
even a Sith Lord couldn't revive "Mortal&nbsp;
Kombat: Annihilation" in the eyes of moviegoers.

00:00:09.298 --> 00:00:15.480
After making a splash in the 1995 "Mortal Kombat,"&nbsp;
film Linden Ashby and Christopher Lambert both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.620
declined to return for "Annihilation," and&nbsp;
were each recast with different actors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.480
In an interview featured in the 2017 book&nbsp;
"Lights, Camera, Game Over!," chronicling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.920
the movie's production, producer Lawrence&nbsp;
Kasanoff attributes a scheduling conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.720
to Lambert dropping out from "Annihilation."&nbsp;
Years after the 1997 film's release, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.667
Lambert cited the poor quality of the&nbsp;
script for his decision to not return.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:41.425
"Ha Ha Ha! … Sorry."

00:00:41.425 --> 00:00:46.020
Kasanoff also claimed that Johnny Cage was killed&nbsp;
early in "Annihilation" to shock the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.500
but Ashby disputes this. In a&nbsp;
separate interview in "Lights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.340
Camera, Game Over!," Ashby claims he was&nbsp;
open to reprising his role as Johnny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.360
but Kasanoff did not want to honor his&nbsp;
sequel deal. After seeing the script for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.680
"Annihilation," Ashby also turned down the&nbsp;
offer due to the poor quality of writing.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.860
Johnny Cage wasn't the only returning fighter&nbsp;
recast for "Annihilation," as J.J. Perry replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.980
Chris Casamassa as Scorpion when Casamassa signed&nbsp;
on to do stunts in "Batman &amp; Robin" instead. More&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.080
than just playing the yellow-clad ninja warrior,&nbsp;
Perry also played Noob Saibot and Cyrax in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.760
film, with each ninja getting their own fights&nbsp;
in the movie. Pulling triple duty came at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.140
painful price for Perry, as the actor and stunt&nbsp;
performer injured himself during production.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.760
Perry revealed that the cumbersome mask involved&nbsp;
in the Cyrax costume inhibited his vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.620
causing Perry to roll his ankle on a power&nbsp;
cable. Because of this injury, Perry says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:42.120
he wasn't at his full physical potential for&nbsp;
Scorpion's fight scene against his icy rival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:47.220
Sub-Zero. In a 2019 interview with&nbsp;
Upcomer.com, Casamassa revealed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.840
the planned fight sequence between Scorpion&nbsp;
and Sub-Zero had originally been designed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.011
be longer and more ambitious, but may have&nbsp;
been shortened after his role was recast.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.380
It wasn't only major cast members who were&nbsp;
replaced from the original "Mortal Kombat."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:07.380
Its director, controversial filmmaker Paul W.S.&nbsp;
Anderson ultimately opted not to helm the sequel.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.480
Anderson made a name for himself in the late&nbsp;
'90s and early 2000s overseeing films that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.640
were often financially successful, such as&nbsp;
genre fare like "Alien vs. Predator." His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.420
decision not to return to "Annihilation"&nbsp;
opened up the director's chair to John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.840
R. Leonetti, the cinematographer&nbsp;
from the first "Mortal Kombat."

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.220
Though Anderson maintains his vocal respect&nbsp;
for Kasanoff, in an interview in "Lights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.620
Camera, Game Over!," he described his dynamic with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.700
the producer as less than smooth during&nbsp;
"Mortal Kombat"'s production. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.908
he voiced his support in having Leonetti&nbsp;
promoted to director for "Annihilation."

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.580
Whereas the 1995 "Mortal Kombat" was&nbsp;
filmed in California and Thailand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.720
"Annihilation" was more ambitious, with&nbsp;
production traveling to a higher number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.420
countries for filming locations during principal&nbsp;
photography. In a 1997 interview with GamePro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.920
Lawrence Kasanoff promised the film would be&nbsp;
bigger in scope and action than its predecessor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.760
This meant there would be more scenes set&nbsp;
within the parallel universe of Outworld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.544
filmed in more exotic locations to create an&nbsp;
otherworldly aesthetic to the proceedings.

00:03:07.544 --> 00:03:13.004
"Your planet and our world have begun to merge&nbsp;
into one realm. Think of it as hell on earth."

00:03:13.004 --> 00:03:17.820
Aside from Thailand, "Annihilation" also&nbsp;
shot scenes on location in Israel, Jordan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.680
and the United Kingdom, with shooting in the&nbsp;
latter location including the abandoned copper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.260
mine Parys Mountain. In a 1997 interview timed&nbsp;
to coincide with the film's release, Kasanoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.000
explained that wet, windy weather conditions&nbsp;
at the copper mine had hampered filming. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.727
one instance, the winds picked up so dramatically&nbsp;
that the production's base camp was blown away.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.660
One of the few actors from the 1995&nbsp;
"Mortal Kombat" film to return for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:47.340
"Annihilation" was Robin Shou, reprising his&nbsp;
fan-favorite role as master martial artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.060
Liu Kang. While Shou performed&nbsp;
many of his own fight scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.420
he did have a stunt performer double&nbsp;
for certain aspects of his performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.160
Doubling for Shou in "Annihilation" was&nbsp;
Thai actor and stunt choreographer Tony Jaa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.940
who would go on to have his own celebrated&nbsp;
career as a martial arts movie star.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:09.120
Jaa became known to international audiences after&nbsp;
starring in the 2003 martial arts film "Ong-Bak:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.660
The Thai Warrior," and he's since&nbsp;
gone on to appear in "Furious 7"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.500
and "Monster Hunter." Though Jaa had&nbsp;
done stunt work in local Thai films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.340
"Annihilation" provided him a major&nbsp;
stepping stone, with Jaa recalling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.507
years later his good fortune in fitting&nbsp;
Shou's physical size and fighting style.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.340
Like Tony Jaa, prolific stunt performer&nbsp;
Ray Park similarly rose from nameless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:33.600
background performer to beloved genre star&nbsp;
shortly after his work in "Mortal Kombat."

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.640
Park had one of his first on-camera experiences&nbsp;
in "Annihilation," and like Perry, he took on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.900
multiple roles to do so. Most notably,&nbsp;
the Scottish stuntman doubled for James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:48.300
Remar as Raiden in various sequences. Park&nbsp;
also doubled for Dennis Keifer as Baraka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.960
and appeared as one of the monstrous Outworld&nbsp;
Reptile ninjas that menace the heroes.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:56.580
Park broke out in 1999, appearing&nbsp;
as both Darth Maul in "Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.720
Episode I — The Phantom Menace" and as the&nbsp;
Headless Horseman in Tim Burton's "Sleepy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.880
Hollow." Park's first major on-screen speaking&nbsp;
role was in 2000's "X-Men," playing the acrobatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.620
mutant supervillain Toad, and he went on to&nbsp;
play Snake Eyes in the early "G.I. Joe" films.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:15.420
Park has since navigated some controversies,&nbsp;
but during a panel appearance at Salt Lake City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.340
Comic-Con in 2013, he credited "Mortal Kombat:&nbsp;
Annihilation" for jump-starting his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.120
and expressed interest in returning&nbsp;
to the franchise if ever invited.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:29.400
Chris Conrad replaced Linden Ashby in the role&nbsp;
of Johnny Cage in "Annihilation". Audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.660
didn't have long to get accustomed to Conrad&nbsp;
in the role, however, as Johnny is brutally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.820
killed by Shao Kahn in the opening scene. As&nbsp;
Conrad recalled to Killerfilm.com in 2011,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.440
"Yep, got my neck broke in record time. I&nbsp;
told people when it came out to 'get your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.040
popcorn and Rasinets after the first&nbsp;
10 minutes, so you don't miss me!'"

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.060
But despite this quick, unceremonious&nbsp;
dispatching of the beloved character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:55.920
tentative plans at the time were to have Johnny&nbsp;
return later in the film as a ghost. Evidently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.445
that's not how things played&nbsp;
out in the film's production.

00:05:58.778 --> 00:06:01.740
There were plans by Kasanoff&nbsp;
to continue the film series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.600
with a sequel to "Annihilation," tentatively&nbsp;
named "Mortal Kombat: Devastation." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.680
the poor critical and commercial reception&nbsp;
of "Annihilation" quietly led to the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:15.420
being delayed for years. In the early 2000s&nbsp;
pre-production picked up again, with both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:19.800
Shou and Ashby saying in interviews at the time&nbsp;
that the film was planning to go into production.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.700
After a planned filming location in&nbsp;
Louisiana was impacted by Hurricane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.720
Katrina and Kasanoff unsuccessfully&nbsp;
sued to stop the sale of Midway Games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.384
and the "Mortal Kombat" license to Warner&nbsp;
Bros., "Devastation" was shelved for good.

00:06:32.384 --> 00:06:36.540
Though Kasanoff envisioned "Annihilation"&nbsp;
going bigger and better than its predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.680
a tight production budget and rushed&nbsp;
schedule complicated matters. This led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.700
to significant sequences that were written&nbsp;
for the film being reduced or cut entirely.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:49.560
In an interview for "Lights, Camera, Game&nbsp;
Over!", co-writer Brent V. Friedman recalled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:53.700
that budget limitations lead to major&nbsp;
cuts from the final screenplay, saying,

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:11.820
Looking back on the disastrous critical&nbsp;
reception and box office drop-off for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:16.560
"Mortal Kombat" sequel, Kasanoff cites a rushed&nbsp;
production schedule as the primary culprit.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.160
In "Lights, Camera, Game Over!", Kasanoff&nbsp;
said he should have insisted the studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.780
wait at least six more months before launching&nbsp;
into Annihilation's production. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:29.520
the breakneck schedule they opted for resulted&nbsp;
in truncated development and post-production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:34.080
cycles. It also required additional material&nbsp;
to be filmed to make things more coherent.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.040
Kasanoff revealed that they had no time&nbsp;
for reshoots to resolve any plot holes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.720
if they were to meet the studio's all-important&nbsp;
deadline. Looking back, Kasanoff explained that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:47.100
would've liked an additional week in Thailand and&nbsp;
Jordan, along with several weeks of studio time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:51.180
to undergo reshoots after reviewing&nbsp;
a rough cut. However, like the issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:55.967
with the post-production cycle, these hopes were&nbsp;
dashed in order to reach the unyielding deadline.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.560
The post-production team for "Annihilation" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.100
spread all over the globe, but despite&nbsp;
the production's global talent reach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.760
the visual effects were still not completed&nbsp;
in time to meet the studio's release date.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.380
This failure to meet the anticipated deadline&nbsp;
did not deter the studio from proceeding with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:14.280
the movie's theatrical release, much to&nbsp;
Kasanoff's chagrin. In "Lights, Camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:18.480
Game Over!", Kasanoff admits that the visual&nbsp;
effects in "Annihilation" remain unfinished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.440
to this day, with the studio deeming&nbsp;
them, quote, "good enough" at the time.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.800
"...Pretty cool huh?"

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.520
With expectations riding high for&nbsp;
"Annihilation" to meet its November&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.660
1997 release date, the studio estimated&nbsp;
the unfinished film would make better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:43.320
business releasing on time than delaying&nbsp;
the premiere to complete its effects.

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:47.760
Though Lambert did not reprise his role&nbsp;
as Raiden for "Annihilation," the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.140
was considered to return to the franchise&nbsp;
for a canceled follow-up. In an August 2016&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:57.000
interview with Loaded, Lambert revealed he had&nbsp;
read a script for a proposed third film and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.300
in contrast to "Annihilation," he&nbsp;
enjoyed the planned sequel's screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:04.500
though it's unclear if the script that Lambert&nbsp;
read was the same one seen by Shou or Ashby.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:08.940
According to Lambert, the next "Mortal Kombat"&nbsp;
film would've bent the rules of space and time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:13.560
with fighters traveling through time to totally&nbsp;
different locations in the midst of battle. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:17.640
planned film may have been part of New Line's&nbsp;
attempts to revive the franchise, as a reboot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:23.280
was announced in 2011 with Kevin Tancharoen&nbsp;
attached to direct. In November 2016, Simon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:28.560
McQuoid was hired to helm what would eventually&nbsp;
become the 2021 "Mortal Kombat" reboot, discarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.847
any plans for a sequel to "Annihilation,"&nbsp;
along with Lambert's potential return.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:37.800
After directing the 1995 "Mortal&nbsp;
Kombat," Paul W.S. Anderson would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:41.700
go on to helm different video game&nbsp;
film adaptations, including "Resident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.780
Evil" and "Monster Hunter." Anderson was&nbsp;
appalled at how the cinematic world he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:49.140
helped introduce in the first "Mortal&nbsp;
Kombat" was diminished by its sequel.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:52.920
This observation would affect how Anderson&nbsp;
approached his management of the "Resident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:56.700
Evil" franchise, with the filmmaker&nbsp;
intent on taking a more hands-on role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:00.600
to ensure a consistent level of quality.&nbsp;
In "Lights, Camera, Game Over!," he shared,

00:10:13.347 --> 00:10:17.591
"Mother - you're alive!"

00:10:17.591 --> 00:10:21.360
"Too bad YOU… will die!"

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:24.840
Anderson produced and wrote every&nbsp;
installment of the "Resident Evil" series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.200
and directed four of the movies.&nbsp;
He has credited at least some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.140
this grand design to the knowledge&nbsp;
of what "Kombat" became, saying,

00:10:35.627 --> 00:10:39.780
The original "Mortal Kombat" video game&nbsp;
was created by Ed Boon and John Tobias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:44.280
with Boon remaining alongside the franchise&nbsp;
throughout its lengthy history. Boon has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:49.260
voiced the character Scorpion since the 1991 game,&nbsp;
reprising his voiceover role as the masked ninja&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:54.600
warrior for both the 1995 film and "Annihilation."&nbsp;
Despite voicing the character in "Annihilation,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.600 --> 00:11:00.240
Boon regards the 1997 film as the franchise's&nbsp;
lowest point, a stance shared by Tobias.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.560
In a 2012 interview for Complex looking&nbsp;
back on the entire history and legacy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:08.640
the "Mortal Kombat" franchise, Boon kept&nbsp;
his analysis succinct, saying he was not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:13.140
fan of "Annihilation." Tobias, meanwhile, is&nbsp;
credited as a contributing co-writer to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:18.060
story of "Annihilation," along with Kasanoff&nbsp;
and Joshua Wexler. Friedman and Bryce Zabel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:22.860
are credited for the screenplay. In an online&nbsp;
Q &amp; A, Tobias similarly listed "Annihilation"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:26.640
as his least favorite "Mortal Kombat"&nbsp;
memory, and recalled seeing rough cuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.540
of the film during production that drove&nbsp;
home a sharp drop in cinematic quality.

